<h2 id="switch-palette">Light and dark palettes</h2>
<p>The header's <strong>Dark mode</strong> button switches the HTML <code>data-theme</code> attribute between <code>dark</code> and <code>light</code>. Dark is the initial default, including when JavaScript or storage is unavailable. A valid saved choice is applied before styles load. The site deliberately does not follow the operating system's color preference; the explicit choice persists locally when storage is available.</p>
<p>In <code>src/my-epic-css/_custom.scss</code>, set <code>$palette-name: "Harbor";</code> (blue), <code>$palette-name: "Dusk";</code> (charcoal/lavender), or <code>$palette-name: "Neon";</code> (black/coral/teal), then rebuild Sass. Dusk uses charcoal-violet surfaces and lavender accents in dark mode, with pale lavender surfaces and deeper violet accents in light mode. Neon uses bright coral and teal on black/charcoal in dark mode, with deeper accents on warm near-white surfaces in light mode. These three presets form the finished set. The runtime button switches light/dark within that compiled preset; there is no runtime palette picker. <code>index.scss</code> imports custom settings before centralized <code>_variables.scss</code>. Keep <code>!default</code> on library values: it means “use this fallback only when no value has been assigned.” The library's purple/green defaults remain separate from the workshop's named presets.</p>
<p>The selected record in the site's <code>$palettes</code> map supplies the eight existing <code>$clr-*</code> defaults and the <code>$color-modes</code> pair. Each named preset contains complete <code>light</code> and <code>dark</code> variants using the same eight roles: <code>body</code>, <code>body-bg</code>, <code>primary</code>, <code>primary-light</code>, <code>primary-bg</code>, <code>secondary</code>, <code>secondary-bg</code> and <code>white</code>. To customize a preset, edit its complete pair inside <code>$palettes</code> and select its exact name with <code>$palette-name</code>. Unknown names or incomplete modes/roles fail compilation. Saved original commented palettes are deliberately retained as references; they do not select the active palette. Every variant keeps the quoted <code>"white": #ffffff</code> role; it is not a semantic body-text alias.</p>
<p>The optional colors module emits default <code>--clr-*</code> properties on <code>:root</code> and mode overrides on <code>html[data-theme="light"]</code> and <code>html[data-theme="dark"]</code>. Existing utilities, base text, links, buttons and SVG <code>currentColor</code> use those properties. Single-palette consumers can leave <code>$color-modes</code> empty. Base rules also have Sass-value fallbacks, so selective imports keep working without the colors module. Import central variables before base or colors.</p>
<p><code>$clr-body</code> sets base text. <code>$clr-primary-light</code> sets the accent on <code>main a.clr-underline:hover</code>. There are no standalone <code>clr-body</code> or <code>clr-primary-light</code> utilities. Changing modes updates semantic values without adding alternate utility names. Literal artwork and demo gradients keep their own colors.</p>
<p>Run <code>npm start</code> after editing Sass. Check text, links, focus outlines, disabled buttons and code examples in both modes. Fixed white text still needs a dark surface. Active light presets adjust some accent colors from the saved original samples for readability; calculated color-pair checks do not establish accessibility for an entire page.</p>
<p>Stop development watchers before <code>npm run build</code> or <code>npm run validate</code>. The build compiles Sass, filters CSS using source classes, and generates the site. Validation uses temporary output and restores CSS inputs. Preview both modes after filtering.</p>
